  "step_2": "The individual considers the 80% probability of winning 4000 Euros as sufficient to justify taking a risk compared to the guaranteed 3000 Euros. The verbal report emphasizes that 80% is enough to 'poker' (take a gamble on) 4000 Euros versus the certain 3000. This indicates that the individual is focusing on the high probability of a larger reward (80% chance of 4000) and is willing to accept a 20% risk of getting nothing. The individual does not mention skewness, distribution shape, or the relative weight of favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes in terms of probability mass. Instead, the justification centers on the adequacy of the 80% probability to make the gamble worthwhile. The individual considers the likelihood of the high outcome and compares it to the certainty of the lower outcome, but not in terms of skewness.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness—specifically, preferring the lottery with more probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes (right-skewed). However, the individual did not consider skewness at all. Instead, the justification was based on the high probability (80%) of a larger payoff, which is a risk-taking attitude toward a high-probability gain, not a preference for right-skewness. In fact, Lottery B is left-skewed, not right-skewed, so the reason would not favor it. The individual’s reasoning aligns more with a probabilistic evaluation of a favorable outcome (high chance of high reward) rather than a concern with the shape of the distribution in terms of skewness. There is no mention of unfavorable outcomes receiving higher probability, nor any reference to the distribution’s tail behavior. Therefore, the decision reason was not used. The individual’s justification is based on a different cognitive mechanism—probability-weighted value or risk tolerance—not on skewness preference.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

